### What is a JPA Entity?

An Entity is annotated with `@Entity` and mapped to a database table. It is managed by the JPA persistence context, meaning changes to its fields inside an open transaction are automatically flushed to the database. It has an identity column, often relationships to other entities, and lifecycle callbacks.

Because it is tied to the schema and the persistence context, you keep it internal. Exposing it risks leaking columns, coupling the API to the schema, and triggering `LazyInitializationException` when a serializer touches an unloaded relationship outside a transaction.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {
    @Id private String id;
    private String holderName;
    @Version private long version;                     // optimistic locking
    private String passwordHash;                       // never expose
    private int internalRiskScore;                     // never expose
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<Transaction> transactions;            // lazy-loaded
    // getters and setters
}
```

### What is a DTO?

A DTO is a plain class, frequently a Java record for immutability, whose purpose is to move data across a boundary. It is not managed by JPA and has no database identity. It commonly carries validation annotations for request shapes and defines the exact fields exposed to clients. It holds no business logic; it just carries data.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record TransferRequest(
        @NotBlank String fromAccount,
        @NotBlank String toAccount,
        @DecimalMin("0.01") BigDecimal amount
) {}
// Validation annotations, no logic, immutable
```

### What is a POJO?

POJO stands for Plain Old Java Object. It is the broadest term: any Java object not tied to a framework. An Entity is technically a POJO with JPA annotations, and a DTO is a POJO used for transport, but when people say POJO specifically, they mean a framework-free domain object that may contain business logic.

```java
package com.bank.domain;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;

public class Money {
    private final BigDecimal amount;
    private final String currency;

    public Money(BigDecimal amount, String currency) {
        if (amount.scale() > 2) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Max 2 decimal places for money");
        }
        this.amount = amount;
        this.currency = currency;
    }

    public Money add(Money other) {
        if (!this.currency.equals(other.currency)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Cannot add different currencies");
        }
        return new Money(this.amount.add(other.amount), this.currency);
    }

    public Money applyInterest(BigDecimal rate) {
        BigDecimal interest = amount.multiply(rate).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
        return new Money(amount.add(interest), currency);
    }
}
```

This `Money` class has real business logic but zero framework coupling, which makes it easy to unit test and reuse anywhere.

### How are Entity, DTO, and POJO different? (comparison table)

| Feature | Entity | DTO | POJO |
|---|---|---|---|
| Framework coupling | JPA (`@Entity`, `@Id`) | Validation (`@NotBlank`) | None |
| Business logic | No (persistence only) | No (transport only) | Yes |
| Database identity | Yes (`@Id`) | No | No |
| Mutability | Mutable (managed) | Typically immutable (record) | Either |
| Where it lives | Persistence layer | API boundary / between layers | Domain layer |
| Exposed to clients | Never | Always | Rarely |

### When do you use each?

The flow in a typical request is: the controller receives a request DTO, the service maps it toward the domain, uses entities for persistence through the repository, performs logic possibly using POJO value objects, then maps an entity to a response DTO to return. The entity never escapes the service boundary.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dto.*;
import com.bank.domain.Money;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {

    public TransferResponse transfer(TransferRequest req) {
        Account from = repository.findById(req.fromAccount()).orElseThrow();
        Account to   = repository.findById(req.toAccount()).orElseThrow();

        // POJO value object for business logic:
        Money amount = new Money(req.amount(), "INR");

        // Entity – persistence concern:
        from.debit(amount.getAmount());
        to.credit(amount.getAmount());
        repository.save(from);
        repository.save(to);

        // DTO – transport concern:
        return new TransferResponse("TXF-001", "COMPLETED", from.getBalance());
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The biggest mistake is collapsing all three into the entity: adding `@NotBlank` validation annotations to the entity, adding `@JsonIgnore` for serialization, and returning it directly from the controller. This couples persistence, transport, and validation into one class.

```java
// Wrong – entity doing all three jobs:
@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id private String id;
    @NotBlank private String holderName;       // transport validation on entity
    @JsonIgnore private String passwordHash;   // serialization concern on entity
    private BigDecimal balance;
}

// Correct – separate responsibilities:
@Entity class Account { /* persistence only */ }
record CreateAccountRequest(@NotBlank String holderName) { /* validation only */ }
record AccountResponse(String id, BigDecimal balance) { /* transport only */ }
```

### What about lazy-loading bugs from exposing entities?

When the controller returns an entity and the JSON serializer runs after the transaction has closed, touching a lazy relationship like `account.getTransactions()` throws `LazyInitializationException`. Mapping to a DTO inside the transaction, copying only the fields you need, avoids this because the serializer never touches the entity.

```java
// This crashes outside a transaction:
@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
public Account get(@PathVariable String id) {
    return repo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    // Jackson touches account.transactions → LazyInitializationException
}

// This works – DTO created inside the service transaction:
@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
public AccountResponse get(@PathVariable String id) {
    return accountService.getAccount(id); // maps to DTO inside transaction
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Keeping the three roles separate is a hallmark of a well-layered banking application. Interviewers look for whether you understand why the separation exists, not just that it does. The entity stays below the service boundary. The DTO crosses the API boundary with validation. The POJO holds domain logic that is pure, testable, and reusable. Tools like MapStruct generate the entity-to-DTO mapping at compile time, so the common objection of too much boilerplate does not hold.

A senior also knows that in a hexagonal or ports-and-adapters architecture, the POJO domain model sits at the center, free of all framework dependencies. The entity is an adapter for persistence, and the DTO is an adapter for the API. This separation lets you swap the persistence technology or the API format without touching the domain logic.
